    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d125">

                <pb facs="0183" n="118" sameAs="frus:frus1936v03/pg118"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/4065: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister Resident in
                        Ethiopia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-04-10T17:00:00-05:00">April 10, 1936—5 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>136. Consul at Geneva in his 105, April 9, 5 p.m.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d125-f1"
                    /> reports in part as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The British here are taking the position that if conciliation does not
                            show immediate satisfactory results they will press for increased <pb
                                facs="0184" n="119" xml:id="pg119"/> sanctions which would be
                            signalized by the convening of the Committee of Eighteen.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>He adds that it appears unlikely that sanction can be extended through auspices
                    of League. It likewise appears that British have not determined upon policy in
                    event of failure of conciliation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
